# Verdict: **REVISE**

## Pre-commitment Predictions

Before reading code, I predicted these issues based on the disclosure/accordion component type:

1. **Missing `role="region"` on expanded panel** — Most common accordion anti-pattern. Without a region role, screen readers don't announce "expanded section" or provide navigation to it.
2. **Missing `aria-labelledby` on panel** — Even with an id and aria-controls reference, the panel has no accessible name linking it back to its trigger button.
3. **Focus management undefined after expand/collapse** — When content appears/disappears, where does focus go? This is often accidental rather than designed.

## Critical Findings

### 1. Expanded panel missing `role="region"` and accessible name — screen readers cannot identify or navigate to the expanded content

- **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyAccordion.jsx:28` — The `<div>` with `id={\`panel-${index}\`}` has no role, no aria-labelledby, no aria-label. It is a plain div that conditionally renders content.
- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)
- **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern requires `role="region"` on the expanded content container with an accessible name tied to the trigger button via `aria-labelledby`.
- **Why this matters**: Without `role="region"`, screen readers treat the panel as a generic `<div>` — no announcement of "expanded section," no navigation landmark, no way for users to jump directly to accordion content. The panel is invisible to assistive technology as a distinct interactive region. Screen reader users hear: button text + state (collapsed/expanded), then when expanded, they encounter unstructured content with no context about what it belongs to.
- **Fix**: Add `role="region"` and `aria-labelledby={\`trigger-${index}\`}` to the panel div. Give the trigger button an id (`id={\`trigger-${index}\`}`). This creates a bidirectional association: the button controls the region, and the region is named by the button.

```jsx
// Trigger button needs an id:
<button
  id={`trigger-${index}`}
  className="accordion-trigger"
  onClick={() => toggleItem(index)}
  aria-expanded={expanded === index}
  aria-controls={`panel-${index}`}
>
  {item.title}
  <span aria-hidden="true">{expanded === index ? '−' : '+'}</span>
</button>

// Panel needs role and aria-labelledby:
<div
  id={`panel-${index}`}
  className="accordion-panel"
  role="region"
  aria-labelledby={`trigger-${index}`}
>
  {item.content}
</div>
```

- **Confidence**: HIGH — This is a well-documented, non-negotiable requirement of the WAI-ARIA Disclosure pattern. Every accordion implementation must include this.

---

## Major Findings

### 2. No focus management design when expanding/collapsing panels

- **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyAccordion.jsx:7-9` — The `toggleItem` function only updates state (`setExpanded`). There is no focus manipulation, no `setTimeout`, no programmatic focus movement.
- **User group impacted**: Keyboard users and screen reader users
- **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order; WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern recommends that when a panel expands, focus should either remain on the trigger (if user is navigating by tab) or move to the newly expanded content (if using arrow keys). When collapsing, focus should return to the trigger.
- **Why this matters**: Without designed focus behavior, keyboard users may lose their place after expanding/collapsing. Screen reader users hear content appear but don't know where focus is relative to it. This is especially problematic in a single-expand accordion (only one panel open at a time) — when the user collapses item 3 and expands item 1, focus behavior becomes ambiguous.
- **Fix**: Implement explicit focus management. After expanding, keep focus on the trigger button (simplest approach). Optionally move focus into the expanded panel content if using arrow key navigation between triggers.

```jsx
const toggleItem = (index) => {
  const wasExpanded = expanded === index;
  setExpanded(wasExpanded ? -1 : index);

  // Keep focus on trigger after state update
  setTimeout(() => {
    document.getElementById(`trigger-${index}`)?.focus();
  }, 0);
};
```

- **Confidence**: HIGH — Focus management is a core requirement of the APG pattern. The current implementation has none.

### 3. No keyboard interaction beyond click (arrow key navigation missing)

- **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyAccordion.jsx:14-20` — Only `onClick` handler exists. No `onKeyDown`, no arrow key handling, no Home/End key support.
- **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users and screen reader users navigating by keyboard
- **WCAG/APG citation**: WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern recommends arrow key navigation between triggers (Up/Down arrows move focus between accordion headers). This is a "should" rather than "must" for simple disclosure widgets, but it significantly improves keyboard usability.
- **Why this matters**: Without arrow key navigation, keyboard users must Tab through all triggers sequentially. In a long accordion with many items, this creates excessive tabbing. Arrow keys provide faster navigation between related controls.
- **Fix**: Add `onKeyDown` handler to each trigger button supporting Up/Down/Home/End arrows for navigation between triggers.

```jsx
const handleKeyDown = (e, index) => {
  const triggerCount = items.length;
  let newIndex;

  switch(e.key) {
    case 'ArrowDown':
      e.preventDefault();
      newIndex = (index + 1) % triggerCount;
      document.getElementById(`trigger-${newIndex}`)?.focus();
      break;
    case 'ArrowUp':
      e.preventDefault();
      newIndex = (index - 1 + triggerCount) % triggerCount;
      document.getElementById(`trigger-${newIndex}`)?.focus();
      break;
    case 'Home':
      e.preventDefault();
      document.getElementById('trigger-0')?.focus();
      break;
    case 'End':
      e.preventDefault();
      document.getElementById(`trigger-${triggerCount - 1}`)?.focus();
      break;
  }
};
```

- **Confidence**: MEDIUM — Arrow key navigation is recommended but not strictly required for simple disclosure widgets. The critical requirement (region role + aria-labelledby) is already missing, making this secondary.

---

## What's Missing

1. **`role="region"` on expanded content** — The most critical gap. Without it, the accordion panel is invisible as a navigable region to assistive technology users.
2. **`aria-labelledby` linking panel to trigger** — Creates bidirectional association between button and content.
3. **Trigger button `id` attribute** — Needed for `aria-labelledby` reference on the panel.
4. **Focus management after expand/collapse** — No designed behavior for where focus goes when state changes.
5. **Arrow key navigation between triggers** — Keyboard users have no fast way to move between accordion items.

---

## Multi-Perspective Notes

- **Screen reader user**: Hears button text + "collapsed" or "expanded." When expanded, encounters unstructured content with no announcement that it's a region belonging to the button they just activated. No way to navigate directly to accordion sections.
- **Keyboard-only user**: Can click triggers (via Enter/Space on buttons — this works). Tab order is logical (buttons in DOM order). But after expanding/collapsing, focus behavior is undefined. No arrow key shortcuts between items.
- **Low vision user**: Button text and +/- indicator are visible. Panel content appears/disappears as expected. Focus indicators depend on browser defaults (not styled here). No color-only state indicators — the +/- icon has `aria-hidden`, which is good.
- **Cognitive accessibility**: Interaction model is simple (click to expand/collapse, only one open at a time). Clear visual indicator (+/-) with programmatic equivalent (`aria-expanded`). Error handling not applicable for this component type.

---

## Verdict Justification

**REVISE** because the accordion implements 60% of the WAI-ARIA Disclosure pattern but is missing its most critical requirement: `role="region"` with `aria-labelledby` on the expanded content panel. Without these, screen reader users cannot identify or navigate to accordion sections — they hear button states but encounter unstructured content without context. This is not a minor omission; it fundamentally breaks the interaction model for assistive technology users.

**To upgrade to ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS**: Add `role="region"` and `aria-labelledby` to panels, add trigger ids, implement basic focus management after state changes.

**To upgrade to ACCEPT**: Implement all of the above plus arrow key navigation between triggers and comprehensive focus restoration design.

---

## Open Questions (unscored)

- Does the accordion need a wrapper with `role="presentation"` or should it remain unstructured? (Depends on surrounding page landmarks.)
- Is there a maximum number of accordion items that would make arrow key navigation essential vs. optional? (Long lists benefit more from keyboard shortcuts.)
- Are accordion panels lazy-loaded, or does all content render in the DOM regardless of expanded state? (If all content renders, `aria-hidden="true"` on collapsed panels is an alternative to conditional rendering — different focus/announcement implications.)
